      "text": "To find the inverse of the composition function \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we need to use the property of inverse functions which states that the inverse of a composition of functions is the composition of the inverses in reverse order. That is, if \\( g \\circ f \\) has an inverse \\( f^{-1} \\circ g^{-1} \\), then \\( g \\circ f \\circ h \\) has an inverse \\( h \\circ f^{-1} \\circ g^{-1} \\).\n\nIn this problem, we can use this property to find the inverse of \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\) as follows:\n\n\\[\nf^{-1} \\circ g \\circ h = h \\circ g^{-1} \\circ f^{-1}\n\\]\n\nLet's apply this to our specific case where \\( g = q \\), \\( h = p \\), and \\( f = r \\):\n\n\\[\nq \\circ p \\circ r\n\\]\n\nTrying to find the inverse \\( r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\):\n\n\\[\nr^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ (q \\circ p \\circ r)\n\\]\n\nSince the \\( r^{-1} \\) will be in the middle, we need to adjust our reasoning. Let's start from the reverse order:\n\n\\[\nf = q \\circ p \\circ r\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the inverse \\( f^{-1} \\). We use:\n\n\\[\nf^{-1} = r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}\n\\]\n\nBy reversing the operation as per the property, we can rearrange the steps in a sense:\n\n\\[\nr^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ (q \\circ p \\circ r)\n\\]\n\nThis corresponds to\n\n\\[\nr^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ (q \\circ r \\circ p)\n\\]\n\nThus, we see that the sequence of operations needs reversing once. So the correct expression for \\( f^{-1} \\) is:\n\n\\[\nr^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}\n\\]\n\nThis is option (B). \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B}\n\\]",
